hypotheses.statement:
For every fixed real $u>0$, $$\label{l:degree-all-powers-equation}
 C_u:=\sup_{n\ge1}\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}D_\lambda^{-u}<\infty,
 \qquad Z_u(n)\longrightarrow0.$$

For $1\le k=k(\lambda)\le n/4$, with $\gamma$ the lower diagram after orienting a longest line as the first row, $$\label{l:degree-first-row-bound}
 D_\lambda\ge e^{-1/2}\binom nkD_\gamma
                  \ge\tfrac12\binom nk.$$

There is an absolute $c_0>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $b$, if $\gamma\vdash b$, $L=\max(\gamma_1,\gamma'_1)$, and $k=b-L>0$, then $$\log D_\gamma\ge
 \begin{cases}
 c_0 k\log(b/k),&k\le b/2,\\
 c_0 b,&k>b/2.
 \end{cases}
 \label{l:palette-degree-bound}$$ In particular $\log D_\gamma\ge c_1k$ for an absolute $c_1>0$.

For every fixed $s>0$, $$\sum_{\substack{\lambda\vdash m\\D_\lambda>1}}
             D_\lambda^{-s}\longrightarrow0
 \qquad(m\longrightarrow\infty).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Partition $X$ into $b$ nonempty sets $M_1,\ldots,M_b$. Put $H_i=H_{M_i}$. Suppose $f$ is a nonnegative measure on $G$ of mass at most one and, for every $g\in G$ and $i$, $$\label{l:main-cap}
 f(gH_i)\le \frac{B}{[G:H_i]}.$$ For every $u>0$ and every irreducible $\rho$ of degree $D$, $$\label{l:main-hs}
 \|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le bBC_uD^{-1+(u+2)/b}.$$ There is also an independent orbit-span proof of the bound $$\label{l:main-orbit}
 \|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
 \le bBC_uD^{-1+(u+2)/b}.$$ No restriction is imposed on block sizes or on the multiplicities in $\rho|_{H_1\times\cdots\times H_b}$.

For every complex mass function $a$ on a finite group $G$, $$|G|\sum_g|a(g)|^2=\sum_{\rho\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(G)}D_\rho\|\widehat a(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2.$$ If $f\ge0$ has mass $z\le1$, then $$\begin{aligned}
 |G|\sum_g|f(g)-zU(g)|^2&=\sum_{\rho\ne\mathbf 1}D_\rho\|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2,\label{l:centered-plancherel}\\
 \left(\sum_g|f(g)-U(g)|\right)^2&\le(1-z)^2+\sum_{\rho\ne\mathbf 1}D_\rho\|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2.\label{l:deficit-plancherel}
\end{aligned}$$ If $f\le\mu$ for a probability $\mu$, its normalization, when $z>0$, is at variation distance at most $1-z$ from $\mu$.

Let $H\le G$, let $f\ge0$ have mass at most one and coset cap $B/[G:H]$, and let $\tau\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(H)$ have degree $a$. For every $\rho\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(G)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 D_\rho\|\widehat f(\rho)P_{\rho,H,\tau}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2&\le B^2a^2,\label{l:coefficient-isotypic}\\
 D_\rho\|\widehat f(\rho)P_{\rho,H,\tau}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2&\le B^2a^4.\label{l:pointwise-isotypic}
\end{aligned}$$ The first bound comes from coefficient projection on each coset; the second comes from a pointwise bound on the character idempotent.

Let $n$ tend to infinity through multiples of a fixed integer $q>3$, and let $\mu$ be probability laws on $S_n$. For a uniform $n/q$-subset $C$, put $H_C=\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}(C)$, fixing its complement pointwise. Suppose $$\mathbb E_C\|\mu_{H_C}-(U_G)_{H_C}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1),
 \qquad \widehat\mu(\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}})=0.$$ There is a conditional probability law $\nu$ with $\|\nu-\mu\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1)$ such that, for every irreducible $\rho$ of degree $D$, $$\label{l:random-op}
 \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le \frac2M\sqrt{2qC_1}\,D^{-(1-3/q)/2},$$ where $M=1-o(1)$ is the retained mass and $C_1$ is from Lemma <a href="#l:degree-basic" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l:degree-basic">2</a>. In particular for $q=8$, $\|\mu^{*8}-U_G\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1)$.

Suppose a diagram has first row of length $a$, lower diagram $\gamma$ of size $k$, and second row of length $c$ (put $c=0$ if $k=0$).*

1.  *Every tableau on a Young subdiagram extends to the whole diagram. For $0\le b\le\min(a,k)$, $$\label{l:tableau-interleave}
     D_\lambda\ge\binom ab,
     \qquad D_\lambda\ge\binom{a+k-c}{k}.$$*


2.  *If $k\le a$, ballot interleavings give $$\label{l:tableau-ballot}
     D_\lambda\ge\binom{a+k}{k}-\binom{a+k}{k-1}
     =\binom{a+k}{k}\frac{a-k+1}{a+1}
     \ge\frac1{k+1}\binom{2k}{k}.$$ In particular a two-row rectangle of width $b$ has $\binom{2b}{b}/(b+1)$ tableaux.*

3.  *If the nonempty diagonals of constant row-plus-column index have sizes $t_1,\ldots,t_q$, then $$\label{l:tableau-diagonal}
     D_\lambda\ge\prod_{j=1}^q t_j!
     \ge2^{n-q},\qquad
     D_\lambda\ge\left(\frac n{qe}\right)^n.$$ Thus width and height at most $r$ give $q\le2r-1$ and $D_\lambda\ge2^{n-2r+1}$.

With the notation of Lemma <a href="#l:tableau-constructions" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l:tableau-constructions">20</a>, let $b_j$ be the number of lower boxes in column $j$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 D_\lambda
 &=\binom{a+k}{k}D_\gamma
       \prod_{j=1}^a\left(1+\frac{b_j}{a-j+1}\right)^{-1}
       \label{l:hook-exact}\\
 &\ge\binom{a+k}{k}D_\gamma
       \exp\left(-\frac{k(1+\log a)}a\right).
       \label{l:hook-rearrangement}
\end{aligned}$$ If $k\le a$, a second lower bound is $$\label{l:hook-short-tail}
 D_\lambda\ge\binom{a+k}{k}D_\gamma
                \exp\left(-\frac{k}{a-k+1}\right).$$

For every fixed real $u>0$, $$\label{l:degree-all-powers-equation}
 C_u:=\sup_{n\ge1}\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}D_\lambda^{-u}<\infty,
 \qquad Z_u(n)\longrightarrow0.$$ Moreover there are absolute constants $c_1,c_2>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$ and every $\lambda\vdash n$, $$\label{l:degree-tail-exponential}
 D_\lambda\ge c_1e^{c_2k(\lambda)}.$$ For each fixed positive $k$, these dimensions tend uniformly to infinity among shapes with $k(\lambda)=k$.

For $1\le k=k(\lambda)\le n/4$, with $\gamma$ the lower diagram after orienting a longest line as the first row, $$\label{l:degree-first-row-bound}
 D_\lambda\ge e^{-1/2}\binom nkD_\gamma
                  \ge\tfrac12\binom nk.$$ For $k>n/4$ and all sufficiently large $n$, $D_\lambda\ge e^{n/100}$. These estimates independently give $Z_1(n)\to0$ and $C_1<\infty$.

Let $G$ be a finite group, $H\le G$, and let $f\ge0$ have mass $z\le1$ and satisfy $f(gH)\le B/[G:H]$ for every $g\in G$. Let $\tau\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(H)$ have degree $d_\tau$. Suppose $J\le H$ has a one-dimensional unitary character $\chi$ occurring in $\tau|_J$, and put $I=[H:J]$. For $\rho\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(G)$ of degree $D$, let $Q_\tau$ be the full $\tau$-isotypic projection in $\rho|_H$. Then $$\label{l:subcharacter-isotypic-bound}
 D\|\widehat f(\rho)Q_\tau\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le BzI d_\tau.$$ All copies of $\tau$ are included in $Q_\tau$.

Let $H_i=\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}(M_i)\le S_n$, $1\le i\le b$, for disjoint nonempty sets $M_i$. Let $f\ge0$ have mass $z\le1$ and suppose $$f(gH_i)\le\frac B{[S_n:H_i]}
       \quad(g\in S_n,\ 1\le i\le b).$$ For every irreducible $\rho$ of degree $D$, $$\label{l:character-lift-bound}
 \|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le BzbC_1D^{-1+1026/b},$$ where $C_1=\sup_{m\ge1}\sum_{\tau\in\mathop{\mathrm{Irr}}(S_m)}d_\tau^{-1}$. More generally, if $a\ge0$ and every type of every $H_i$ has a one-dimensional subcharacter of index at most $d_\tau^a$, the exponent $1026/b$ in <a href="#l:character-lift-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="l:character-lift-bound">[l:character-lift-bound]</a> may be replaced by $(a+2)/b$.

Put $k=n-\max(\lambda_1,\lambda'_1)$ and $M(k)=\sum_{j=0}^kp(j)$, where $p(0)=1$ and $p(j)$ is the partition number. Then $$\label{l:joint-types-bound}
 K_\lambda\le M(k)^b.$$ For every fixed $b$ and every fixed $\eta>0$, $$\label{l:joint-types-summability}
 \sum_{\lambda\vdash n:\,D_\lambda>1}
         K_\lambda^2D_\lambda^{-\eta}\longrightarrow0.$$ The statement is uniform over the disjoint sets $M_i$ and does not require them to have equal sizes.

Let $f\ge0$ be a mass function on $S_n$, of total mass $z\le1$, such that $f(gH_i)\le B/[S_n:H_i]$ for every $g,i$. With $K_\lambda$ as in <a href="#l:joint-decomposition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="l:joint-decomposition">[l:joint-decomposition]</a>, $$\label{l:joint-type-operator-bound}
 \|\widehat f(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
       \le\sqrt{BzK_\lambda}\,D_\lambda^{-1/2+1/b}.$$

Let $H_1,\ldots,H_b$ be the disjoint symmetric block subgroups above. Let $\alpha,\beta\ge0$ have masses $a,c\le1$ and satisfy $$\label{l:two-sided-caps}
 \alpha(H_i g)\le\frac{B_L}{[S_n:H_i]},\qquad
 \beta(gH_i)\le\frac{B_R}{[S_n:H_i]}
       \quad(g\in S_n,\ 1\le i\le b).$$ Then every irreducible $\lambda\vdash n$ satisfies $$\label{l:two-sided-bound}
 D_\lambda\|\widehat\beta(\lambda)
                     \widehat\alpha(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le B_LB_Rac\,K_\lambda^2D_\lambda^{-1+4/b}.$$ In particular, for eight blocks and $B_L=B_R=2$, the bound is $4K_\lambda^2D_\lambda^{-1/2}$.

Let $n	o\infty$ through multiples of $256$, and let $\mu_n$ be probabilities on $S_n$ satisfying $$\delta_n=\mathbb E_{|R|=n/256}
       \|(\mu_n)_{-R}-U_{-R}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\longrightarrow0.$$ Condition on the event that at least $7/8$ of the sets $R$ satisfy $\mu_n(gH_R)\le2/[S_n:H_R]$, and call the resulting law $
u_n$. For all sufficiently large $n$ it is defined, satisfies $\|
u_n-\mu_n\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le16\delta_n$, and obeys $$\|\widehat
u_n(ho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le CD^{-1/8}
 \qquad(D=\dimho>1)$$ with an absolute $C$. If $\mathbb E_{\mu_n}\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}(g)=o(1)$, then $\|\mu_n^{*32}-U_n\|_{\mathrm{TV}}	o0$. The averaged relative-entropy hypothesis $$\mathbb E_{|R|=n/256}D((\mu_n)_{-R}\|U_{-R})=o(1)$$ is sufficient for these conclusions.

There is an absolute $c_0>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $b$, if $\gamma\vdash b$, $L=\max(\gamma_1,\gamma'_1)$, and $k=b-L>0$, then $$\log D_\gamma\ge
 \begin{cases}
 c_0 k\log(b/k),&k\le b/2,\\
 c_0 b,&k>b/2.
 \end{cases}
 \label{l:palette-degree-bound}$$ In particular $\log D_\gamma\ge c_1k$ for an absolute $c_1>0$.

Let $n$ tend to infinity through even integers. For each $n$, partition $[n]=A\sqcup B$ into equal halves and let $\mu$ be a probability on $S_n$. Suppose that $\varepsilon_n\to0$ and that, for every palette on either half, every placement of its classes, its subgroup $H$, and every $x\in S_n$, $$\mu(xH)\le [S_n:H]^{-1}
            \exp\{v\mathcal H(p)+\varepsilon_n\}.
 \label{l:palette-transfer-hyp}$$ Here $\delta$ and $v$ satisfy <a href="#l:palette-delta" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="l:palette-delta">[l:palette-delta]</a>. For all sufficiently large $n$, uniformly over $\lambda\notin\{(n),(1^n)\}$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|\widehat\mu(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 &\le D_\lambda^{-1/3},\label{l:palette-fourier-hs}\\
 \|\widehat\mu(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 &\le D_\lambda^{-1/6}.
 \label{l:palette-fourier}
\end{aligned}$$ If in addition $|\widehat\mu(\mathrm{sgn})|=o(1)$, then $\|\mu^{*4}-U_{S_n}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1)$, and consequently $\|\mu^{*30}-U_{S_n}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1)$ as well. Both conclusions also hold with $\mu$ replaced in every factor by a probability $\mu_0$ satisfying $\|\mu_0-\mu\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1)$.

Partition $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ into $q\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $H_i$ be the subgroup supported on block $i$. Suppose a probability $w$ on $S_n$ satisfies $$\label{l:replica-entropy-assumption}
 \sum_{i=1}^qD(w_{H_i}\Vert U_{G/H_i})\le K.$$ For every $t>0$ there is a subprobability $v\le w$, of mass $m$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 1-m&\le\frac{K+q/e}{t},\label{l:replica-mass}\\
 v(gH_i)&\le e^t\frac{|H_i|}{|G|}\quad(g\in G,\ 1\le i\le q),
 \label{l:replica-cap}\\
 \sum_{h\in H_i}(\check v*v)(h)
 &\le e^t\frac{|H_i|}{|G|},\label{l:replica-collision}\\
 \|\widehat v(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 &\le qC_u e^tD_\lambda^{-1+(u+2)/q}
        \quad(\lambda\vdash n, u>0).
 \label{l:replica-hs}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $\check v(g)=v(g^{-1})$, $C_u$ is the constant in Theorem <a href="#l:degree-all-powers" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l:degree-all-powers">22</a>, the Fourier transform is the mass transform $\widehat v(\lambda)=\sum_gv(g)\rho_\lambda(g)$, and the Hilbert–Schmidt norm is unnormalized.

Let $n=2^d$ and suppose, for each sufficiently large integer $d$, that a probability $w$ has an equal partition into $q=256$ blocks with total complement entropy $O(d^4)$. There is $v\le w$ of mass $1-O(d^{-2})$ such that $$\label{l:replica-34-exponent}
 \|\widehat v(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le256 C_{32}e^{d^6}D_\lambda^{-1+34/256}.$$ In particular, whenever $\log D_\lambda>d^8$, this is at most $D_\lambda^{-1/2}$ for all sufficiently large $d$, uniformly in $\lambda$.

Let $\nu$ be any probability on $S_n$ satisfying the sixteen coset caps in <a href="#l:forest-caps" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="l:forest-caps">[l:forest-caps]</a>. Uniformly over all partitions with $$k=n-\lambda_1>n^{1/20},\qquad \lambda'_1\le n/2,$$ one has, for sufficiently large $n$, $$\|\widehat\nu(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le D_\lambda^{-1/3}.
 \label{l:forest-bulk-bound}$$

Let $n\to\infty$ through multiples of $16$. For each $n$, let $\mu$ and $K$ be probabilities on $S_n$. Suppose a fixed equal sixteen-block partition satisfies <a href="#l:forest-entropy-hyp" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="l:forest-entropy-hyp">[l:forest-entropy-hyp]</a> for $\mu$. Suppose also that $K$ satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \|\widehat K(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 &\le n^{-k/10}
 &&\left(1\le k=n-\lambda_1\le n^{1/20}\right),
 \label{l:forest-sparse-hyp}\\
 \widehat K(\lambda)&=0
 &&\left(\lambda'_1>n/2\right).
 \label{l:forest-row-hyp}
\end{aligned}$$ Then the retained probability $\nu$ from Lemma <a href="#l:forest-clipping" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l:forest-clipping">48</a> satisfies $$\|\nu^{*12}*K^{*40}-U_{S_n}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1),
 \qquad
 \|\mu^{*12}*K^{*40}-U_{S_n}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=o(1).
 \label{l:forest-hybrid-conclusion}$$ All factors in these convolutions are independent. There is no assumed identity between $K$ and a convolution root or power of $\mu$.

Put $C_0=2{,}000{,}000$, and fix an integer $L\ge 2C_0$. Let $V=A\sqcup C$, where $|A|=|C|=h$ and $L$ divides $h$. Partition each half into $L$ buffers of size $h/L$, and put $H=S_A\times S_C\subseteq S_V$. Let $\xi$ be a subprobability on $S_V$ such that, for $J=A,C$ and every ordered injection $\mathbf v:J\longrightarrow V$, $$\xi\{x:x^{-1}|_J=\mathbf v\}
 \le 2U_V\{x:x^{-1}|_J=\mathbf v\}.
 \label{l:tabloid-inverse-caps}$$ Let $Z$ have any probability distribution. Measurably in $z$, let $t_z\in S_V$ and let $\nu_A^z,\nu_C^z$ be subprobabilities on $S_A,S_C$, respectively. Suppose that for almost every $z$, each $J=A,C$, each of its buffers $B$, and every ordered injection $\mathbf w:J\setminus B\longrightarrow J$, $$\nu_J^z\{y:y|_{J\setminus B}=\mathbf w\}
 \le 2U_J\{y:y|_{J\setminus B}=\mathbf w\}.
 \label{l:tabloid-factor-caps}$$ Regard the product $\nu_A^z\otimes\nu_C^z$ as a measure on $H$ and set $$\omega=\mathbb E_Z\bigl[\delta_{t_Z}*
                (\nu_A^Z\otimes\nu_C^Z)\bigr],
 \qquad \theta=\omega*\xi.$$ Then, for all sufficiently large $h$, every irreducible representation of $S_V$ with $D_\lambda>1$ satisfies $$\|\widehat\theta(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le K_0D_\lambda^{-1/40},\qquad K_0=\sqrt{22}.
 \label{l:tabloid-product-bound}$$ The threshold on $h$ and the constant $K_0$ are independent of the environment, the transporters and all the measures.

Identify $V$ with $\{1,\ldots,2h\}$, and consider a sequence of the data of Theorem <a href="#l:tabloid-product" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l:tabloid-product">52</a> with $h\to\infty$ and fixed $L$. Suppose that $\mu$ is a probability on $S_{2h}$ satisfying $\theta\le\mu$ pointwise, $$\theta(S_{2h})=1-o(1),\qquad
 \left|\widehat\mu(\mathrm{sgn})\right|=o(1).$$ Then $$\|\mu^{*100}-U_{\{1,\ldots,2h\}}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \longrightarrow0.$$ In particular the sign hypothesis holds if $\mu$ has exactly zero expected sign.

For every fixed $s>0$, $$\sum_{\substack{\lambda\vdash m\\D_\lambda>1}}
             D_\lambda^{-s}\longrightarrow0
 \qquad(m\longrightarrow\infty).$$ In particular $\sum_{\lambda\vdash m}D_\lambda^{-1}\le3$ for all sufficiently large $m$.

Let $b=2^{22}$ and partition $n=bm$ points into $b$ sets of size $m$. Let $H_i$ permute the $i$th set. If $f\ge0$ has mass at most one and $f(gH_i)\le2/[S_n:H_i]$ for every $g,i$, then for all sufficiently large $m$ and every irreducible of degree $D$, $$\label{l:peeling-lift-bound}
 \|\widehat f(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le6bD^{-1+(C_{\rm peel}+2)/b}.$$
